{
    include "registries/aaosa.hocon",
    include "registries/expertise_scoping_instructions.hocon",

    "metadata": {
        "description": "Automated Enterprise Release-Readiness & Code Review network. Evaluates a pull request or repository link, fans out to a Security Auditor, Architecture Concierge, and QA Validator in parallel, and compiles their findings into a Go/No-Go readiness report.",
        "tags": ["devops", "release-management", "security", "AAOSA"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "Is https://github.com/my-org/my-service ready to ship to production?",
            "Review pull request #482 on the payments-service repo for release readiness.",
            "Give me a Go/No-Go report for the latest commit on the main branch."
        ]
    },

    # Load the shared LLM configuration from a single source of truth.
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",

    "tools": [
        # This first agent definition is regarded as the "Front Man", which
        # does all the talking to the outside world/client.
        #
        # Some disqualifications from being a front man:
        #   1) Cannot use a CodedTool "class" definition
        #   2) Cannot use a Tool "toolbox" definition
        {
            "name": "release_manager",
            "function": {
                "description": """
I am the Release Manager. Give me a pull request link or a repository reference (and optionally a branch/commit)
and I will kick off parallel security, architecture, and QA reviews, then issue a Go/No-Go readiness report.
"""
            },
            "instructions": """
You are the Release Manager, the single point of contact for release-readiness requests.
A user will give you a pull request URL or a repository path/reference (and optionally a branch, commit SHA,
or local checkout path). Do the following:
1. Confirm you have enough information to identify the repository (and branch/commit if relevant). Ask one
   clarifying question at a time if something is missing.
2. Call `security_auditor`, `architecture_concierge`, and `qa_validator` with the same repository reference.
   Always call all three, even if you suspect one area is not relevant -- you need a full picture.
3. Wait for all three down-chain agents to respond.
4. Compile their findings into a single "Go/No-Go Readiness Report" with these sections:
   - Overall verdict: GO, NO-GO, or GO WITH CONDITIONS
   - Security findings (from security_auditor)
   - Architecture findings (from architecture_concierge)
   - QA / test coverage findings (from qa_validator)
   - Blocking issues (must fix before release)
   - Non-blocking recommendations
5. The verdict is NO-GO if security_auditor reports any critical/high findings, architecture_concierge reports a
   hard violation of required structure, or qa_validator reports coverage below the required threshold. Otherwise
   GO, or GO WITH CONDITIONS if there are only medium/low findings.
When interacting with the user, act as the single point of interaction. No need to mention internal agent names.
""" ${expertise_scoping_instructions} ${aaosa_instructions},
            "tools": ["security_auditor", "architecture_concierge", "qa_validator"]
        },

        # --- Agent 1: Security Auditor -----------------------------------------------------
        {
            "name": "security_auditor",
            "function": ${aaosa_call}{
                "description": "Scans a repository for hardcoded secrets, vulnerable package dependencies, and OWASP compliance issues."
            },
            "instructions": """
You are the Security Auditor. Given a repository reference, use your tools to:
1. Call `SecretScannerTool` to look for hardcoded secrets/credentials in the source tree.
2. Call `VulnerabilityScannerTool` to check dependency manifests for known-vulnerable package versions.
Summarize findings by severity (critical/high/medium/low) and list the OWASP Top 10 categories implicated
(e.g. A02:2021 Cryptographic Failures, A06:2021 Vulnerable and Outdated Components, A07:2021 Identification
and Authentication Failures). Be specific about file paths and line numbers when available.
""" ${aaosa_instructions},
            "tools": ["SecretScannerTool", "VulnerabilityScannerTool"]
        },
        {
            "name": "SecretScannerTool",
            "function": {
                "description": "A CodedTool that recursively scans a repository path for hardcoded secrets (API keys, passwords, tokens, private keys) using pattern matching.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "repo_path": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Absolute or relative filesystem path to the repository (or sub-directory) to scan."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["repo_path"]
                }
            },
            "class": "release_readiness.secret_scanner_tool.SecretScannerTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "VulnerabilityScannerTool",
            "function": {
                "description": "A CodedTool that inspects dependency manifests (requirements.txt, package.json, pom.xml) for known-vulnerable package versions.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "repo_path": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Absolute or relative filesystem path to the repository (or sub-directory) to scan for dependency manifests."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["repo_path"]
                }
            },
            "class": "release_readiness.vulnerability_scanner_tool.VulnerabilityScannerTool"
        },

        # --- Agent 2: Architecture Concierge ------------------------------------------------
        {
            "name": "architecture_concierge",
            "function": ${aaosa_call}{
                "description": "Compares the repository's code structure against internal engineering documentation guidelines."
            },
            "instructions": """
You are the Architecture Concierge. Given a repository reference, call `ArchitectureComplianceTool` to walk the
file tree and compare it against the internal engineering structure guidelines. Report any missing required
directories/files, any disallowed top-level clutter, and whether the layout matches the expected module
boundaries. Classify each finding as a hard violation (blocking) or a soft recommendation (non-blocking).
""" ${aaosa_instructions},
            "tools": ["ArchitectureComplianceTool"]
        },
        {
            "name": "ArchitectureComplianceTool",
            "function": {
                "description": "A CodedTool that parses the repository's raw file tree and compares it against configured engineering structure guidelines.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "repo_path": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Absolute or relative filesystem path to the repository to evaluate."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["repo_path"]
                }
            },
            "class": "release_readiness.architecture_compliance_tool.ArchitectureComplianceTool"
        },

        # --- Agent 3: QA Validator -----------------------------------------------------------
        {
            "name": "qa_validator",
            "function": ${aaosa_call}{
                "description": "Reviews test execution logs/coverage reports to verify code coverage thresholds and test health."
            },
            "instructions": """
You are the QA Validator. Given a repository reference (and optionally a path to a coverage/test report), call
`TestCoverageTool` to parse the most recent test execution results and coverage report. Report the overall
coverage percentage, pass/fail counts, any flaky or skipped tests, and whether coverage meets the required
threshold (default 80% line coverage unless told otherwise).
""" ${aaosa_instructions},
            "tools": ["TestCoverageTool"]
        },
        {
            "name": "TestCoverageTool",
            "function": {
                "description": "A CodedTool that parses test execution logs / coverage reports (e.g. coverage.xml, junit XML, or plain text summaries) and checks them against a coverage threshold.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "repo_path": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Absolute or relative filesystem path to the repository to search for test/coverage reports."
                        },
                        "coverage_threshold": {
                            "type": "number",
                            "description": "Minimum required line coverage percentage. Defaults to 80 if not specified."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["repo_path"]
                }
            },
            "class": "release_readiness.test_coverage_tool.TestCoverageTool"
        }
    ]
}
